'''''Aquilegia coerulea''''', commonly called the '''Colorado columbine''', '''Rocky Mountain columbine''', or '''blue columbine''', is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the [[buttercup family]], native to the [[Rocky Mountains]] in the western United States. It is a moderate sized wildflower that grows in mountainous areas from the foothills to above [[tree line|timberline]]. The flowers are mainly pollinated by [[bumblebee]]s and several species of [[hawkmoth]]s. The species has been the [[List of U.S. state flowers|state flower]] of [[Colorado]] since 1899 and with its large, showy flowers is a frequent garden plant and used in the breeding of [[Columbine (plant)|columbine]] hybrids. The [[botanical name]] ''coerulea'' (or ''caerulea'') means 'sky blue'.

==Description==
''Aquilegia coerulea'' is a [[herbaceous plant]] with flowering stems that may be {{convert|15–80|cm|in|0|sp=us}} when fully grown.{{sfn|Heil et al. 2013|p=881}} They are perennial plants that typically live for four to six years;{{sfn|LBJWC Staff|2026}} regrowing from a simple or branched [[rootstock]] that is covered in the bases of old [[Petiole (botany)|petioles]], the stems that support individual leaves.{{sfn|Holmgren et al. 2012|p=65}} The [[basal leaves]], the ones growing from the base of the plant, are always shorter than the flowering stems, just {{cvt|9–37|cm|in|round=0.5}} tall. They are [[compound leaves]] that are usually [[biternate]], having nine leaflets in total in groups of three. However, occasionally they may be simply [[trifoliate]], divided into just three leaflets, or [[tripinnate]] with each leaf divided to three levels with 21 leaflets in total.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} Each leaflet is quite thin and has three {{plantgloss|lobe|lobes}}. On their upper sides they are green and {{plantgloss|glabrous}}, smooth and hairless. They may either be glabrous or {{plantgloss|pubescent}}, covered in fine, minute hairs, on the undersides.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=77}}{{sfn|Duft|Moseley|1989|p=68}} Leaflets most often range in size from {{cvt|1.3–4.2|cm|in|1}}, but occasionally may be as long as {{cvt|6.1|cm|in|1}}.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}}

In the wild, blooming can commence as early as late April and might occur as late as early September, though usually plants are not found in bloom earlier than May.{{sfn|Holmgren et al. 2012|p=65}} Mature plants grow one to ten flowering stems, though averaging three to four, each with three to four flowers. The flower furthest out on a stem opens first and then each opens in sequence downward.{{sfn|Brunet|1996|p=2459}} The plant flower buds nod, facing somewhat downward.{{sfn|Art|1990|p=108}} The flowers are large and showy with a diameter of as much as {{convert|15|cm|in|0|sp=us}},{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=77}} but more often about {{cvt|5–10|cm|in|round=0.5}}.{{sfn|Pesman|1988|p=55}} Flowers tend to be smaller on plants growing at higher elevations.{{sfn|Harrington|1964|p=241}} They are so striking that the botanists [[E. O. Wooton]] and [[Paul C. Standley]] enthusiastically wrote of them in their 1915 ''Flora of New Mexico'',{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=77}} {{blockquote|"This is the State flower of Colorado and no other State has one so beautiful. Few indeed are the flowers of the Rockies that can compare with this in beauty. The great blossoms, sometimes six inches in diameter, look like bits of fallen sky, and when the plants cover acres of meadow, as they sometimes do, no words can be found to do them justice."{{sfn|Wooton|Standley|1915|pp=248–249}}}} The flowers are atop {{plantgloss|erect}} stems, ones that stand perpendicular to the ground, and have five large, spreading [[sepal]]s that resemble petals in shades of white to deep-blue or occasionally pink in their wild condition.{{sfn|Heil et al. 2013|p=881}} The sepals range in shape from a flattened circle with somewhat pointed ends with the widest part moved toward the base ({{plantgloss|elliptical|elliptic}}-{{plantgloss|ovate}}) to like a somewhat egg shaped spear head ({{plantgloss|lanceolate|lance}}-ovate). They range in size from 2.6 to 5.1&nbsp;cenimeters long and 0.8–2.3&nbsp;cm.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}}

[[File:Aquilegia coerulea var. coerulea - Brian Finzel 01.jpg|thumb|Variety ''coerulea'' side view showing spurs, photographed near [[Rabbit Ears Pass]]]]
The five petals have very long tapered spurs,{{sfn|Shaw|2004|p=14}} ranging from 2.5 to 7.2&nbsp;centimeters in length, though usually longer than 3.4&nbsp;cm.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}}{{sfn|Heil et al. 2013|p=881}} The spurs are almost straight, but curve outwards slightly and end in a small knob at the tip.{{sfn|Pizzetti|Cocker|1975|p=76}} The blades of the petals are 1.3–2.8&nbsp;cm long by 0.5–1.4&nbsp;cm wide.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} Petals are most often white to cream in color at the front with the spurs having the same range of colors as the sepals.{{sfn|Craighead|Craighead|Davis|1963|p=55}}{{sfn|USFS}} The flowers are scented and produce more volatile molecules during the day than in the evening.{{sfn|Keefover-Ring et al. 2022|p=684}}

The center of the flower has a projecting cluster of numerous bright orange-yellow [[stamen]]s, 50–130 total.{{sfn|Shaw|2004|p=14}}{{sfn|Brunet|2009|p=1568}} The flowers are [[Sequential hermaphroditism#Dichogamy in flowering plants|protandrous]], developing pollen before the [[Stigma (botany)|stigma]] becomes receptive.{{sfn|Brunet|1996|p=2459}} The stamens range in length from 1.3 to 2.4&nbsp;cm.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} They surround three to ten unfused [[carpel]]s with flowers most frequently having six, but varying even on the same plant.{{sfn|Walker|Greene|1932}} If fertilized each of the carpels may develop into a {{plantgloss|follicle}}, a dry pod that splits along one side.{{sfn|Brunet|2009|p=1568}} Each of the pods is round in cross section and is filled with small, smooth, black seeds.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020a}} Both the number of pods that develop and the number of seeds in the pods decrease for flowers that bloom later on a plant.{{sfn|Brunet|1996|p=2468}}

The leaves may be mistaken for that of a [[meadowrue]] (''Thalictrum''), but the flowers are entirely different and the species cannot be confused while flowering.{{sfn|Craighead|Craighead|Davis|1963|p=55}} Similar columbines in its range include [[rock columbine]] (''Aquilegia scopulorum''), [[blue limestone columbine]] (''Aquilegia jonesii''), [[Mancos columbine]] (''Aquilegia micrantha''),{{sfn|Whittemore 2020a}} and [[dwarf blue columbine]] (''Aquilegia saximontana'').{{sfn|Duft|Moseley|1989|p=68}} Rock columbine has leaves that are covered in natural waxes making them blue-green ({{plantgloss|glaucous}}) rather than green. It is found in Nevada and Utah. Blue limestone columbine has much shorter spurs on its flowers, just 0.8–1.5&nbsp;cm and grows to the north in Wyoming, Montana, and Alberta. Mancos columbine has {{plantgloss|viscid}}, very sticky, leaves.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020a}} {{sfn|Duft|Moseley|1989|p=68}} Dwarf blue columbine is only found at very high elevations in rocky areas and has strongly hooked spurs about {{Cvt|2|cm|1}} long as well as being a smaller plant.{{sfn|Duft|Moseley|1989|p=68}}

