===Varieties===
There are four widely accepted varieties of ''Aquilegia coerulea'' according to [[Plants of the World Online]],{{sfn|POWO|2025a}} [[World Flora Online]],{{sfn|WFO 2025}} and World Plants.{{sfn|Hassler|2025}} Though World Plants additionally lists ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''daileyae'' {{small|Eastw.}} as valid as does the USDA [[Natural Resources Conservation Service]] (NRCS) PLANTS database.{{sfn|Hassler|2025}}{{sfn|NRCS 2025c}} However, in the ''Intermountain Flora'' Volume Two, Part A published in 2012 the authors only recognized two varieties, ''coerulea'' and ''ochroleuca''. They wrote of the other varieties, "To us, the sizes of the spurs, stamens, and petals are too variable to sort into meaningful varieties."{{sfn|Holmgren et al. 2012|p=66}}

====''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''alpina''====
This variety was scientifically described by the botanist [[Aven Nelson]] in 1896.{{sfn|POWO|2025b}} It is known by the common name of Colorado alpine columbine.{{sfn|NatureServe 2025b}} Though botanical writers such as Robert Nold doubt the validity of the variety.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=78}} It differs from the other varieties by having much smaller petal blades, only 1.3–1.7&nbsp;centimeters instead of 1.9–2.8&nbsp;centimeters.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} It grows on open rocky slopes in the state of Wyoming at elevations of {{convert|2100-3500|m|ft|sp=us|-2}}. It always has pale blue sepals and flowers from June to August.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020c}} [[NatureServe]] has not evaluated the variety since 2001, but at that time they listed it as vulnerable (T3), but they also list it as possibly [[extirpated]] from Wyoming and with an unevaluated status in Utah.{{sfn|NatureServe 2025b}}

====''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''coerulea''====
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea var. coerulea 01.jpg|thumb|''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''coerulea'' photographed in [[Clear Creek County, Colorado]] ]]
Five species were described at various times that are considered [[heterotypic synonym]]s of this variety.{{sfn|POWO|2025c}} According to the [[Flora of North America]] the [[Autonym (botany)|autonymic]] variety of the species only grows in Wyoming, New Mexico, and Colorado at elevations of {{convert|2100-3500|m|ft|sp=us|-2}}.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020d}} However, the NRCS PLANTS database lists it in the states of Idaho, Montana, and South Dakota, without giving any specific information about the locations where it grows.{{sfn|NRCS 2025b}}

It is distinguished from the two following varieties by having medium to deep blue sepals where they have white, pale blue, or pink sepals.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} Its sepals range in length from 2.8 to 5.1&nbsp;centimeters.{{sfn|Heil et al. 2013|p=881}} The petals have spurs that are 3.4–4.8&nbsp;cm long while their blades are usually 2.0–2.4&nbsp;cm, though occasionally as short as 1.7&nbsp;cm. They grow on rocky slopes, near streams, in open woodlands, and in herb dominated meadows. They flower in the summer starting in mid-June and may flower as late as early September.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020d}} NatureServe evaluated the variety in 2004 and found it to be apparently secure (T4) globally and in Wyoming. They did not evaluate the rest of its range.{{sfn|NatureServe 2025c}}

====''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''daileyae''====
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea var. daileyae - Owen Strickland 01.jpg|thumb|Spurless columbine photographed south of [[Estes Park, Colorado]] ]]
This disputed variety was described by the botanist [[Alice Eastwood]] in 1897.{{sfn|POWO|2025c}} It is a variation where the petals lack spurs and both the petals and spurs are colored deep blue and commonly called '''spurless columbine''',{{sfn|Schneider n.d.}} but is also sometimes called '''Dailey's columbine''' or '''star-flowered columbine'''.{{sfn|NRCS 2025c}}{{sfn|Nelson|1953|p=75}} Most species of columbine occasionally produce these 'stellate' forms.{{sfn|Payson|1918|p=153}} The spurless columbine may be found in identical habitats to var. ''coerulea'', but only in the state of Colorado. From the foothills to alpine areas forest openings.{{sfn|Guennel|1995|p=325}} Part of the reason for its persistances is a lower level of the flowers being eaten by various herbivores such as deer, caterpillars, and aphids.{{sfn|Cabin et al. 2022|p=1334}}

Colorado resident Anna Dailey collected specimens of the spurless columbine near [[Evergreen, Colorado]] and sent them to Eastwood. A genetic mutation causes the petals to be replaced with a second set of sepals. The population continues to reproduce in and near the Reynolds Park open space in [[Jefferson County, Colorado]].{{sfn|Cabin|2019|pp=24–26}} They are also found around [[Estes Park, Colorado]] near [[Rocky Mountain National Park]].{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=79}} Though lacking parts to produce nectar the flowers are still pollinated by bumblebees, which collect pollen from the plants.{{sfn|Cabin|2019|pp=24–26}} When NatureServe evaluated it in 1991 as a variety they rated it as imperiled (T2).{{sfn|NatureServe 2025d}}

====''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''ochroleuca''====
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea var. ochroleuca - Whitney Brook Matson 01.jpg|thumb|''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''ochroleuca'', a white flowered form photographed in [[Teton County, Wyoming]]]]
[[William Jackson Hooker]] described this variety in 1864. It had previously been described as a species by [[Thomas Nuttall]] in 1834 with the name ''Aquilegia leptoceras''. This species name was also unnecessarily reclassified as ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''leptoceras'' by Aven Nelson in 1909. It was inadvertently described by [[Asa Gray]] as ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''albiflora'' in 1895 from which it was reclassified by [[Edwin Blake Payson]] as a [[subspecies]] in 1918.{{sfn|POWO|2025d}} It was not recognized as a distinct variety of the species in ''A Utah Flora'' in 1987 and by Alan T. Whittemore in 1997.{{sfn|Nold|2003|pp=78–79}}

It differs from var. ''coerulea'' by having lighter blue or white sepals (occasionally pink) and from var. ''pinetorum'' by having shorter flower spurs (usually 4–4.8&nbsp;cm, but possibly 3.6–5.4&nbsp;cm) and shorter stamens (1.3–1.8&nbsp;cm).{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} It grows in Idaho, Montana, Nevada, Utah, and Wyoming at elevations of {{convert|2000-3600|m|ft|sp=us|-2}}. It flowers as early as late June and may bloom as late as August.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020e}} It is commonly known as '''white Colorado columbine''', despite the fact that it may have light blue or pink sepals. When it was evaluated by NatureServe in 2004, they assigned it the status of apparently secure (T4).{{sfn|NatureServe 2025e}}

====''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''pinetorum''====
This variety was first described as a species with the name ''Aquilegia pinetorum'' in 1910 by [[Ivar Tidestrom]].{{sfn|POWO|2025e}} The type specimen was collected on the [[Kaibab Plateau]] in [[Coconino County, Arizona]].{{sfn|Kearney|Peebles|1960|p=307}} In 1918 Edwin Blake Payson described it as subspecies of ''Aquilegia coerulea'' and in 1942 [[Thomas Henry Kearney]] and [[Robert Hibbs Peebles]] gave it the present classification as ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''pinetorum''.{{sfn|POWO|2025e}}

Like var. ''ochroleuca'' it may have white to pale blue sepals, occasionally light pink, while it has longer petal spurs (usually 5–5.8&nbsp;cm, but ranging from 4.5 to 7.2&nbsp;cm) and longer flower stamens (1.7–2.4&nbsp;cm).{{sfn|Whittemore 2020b}} This variety is found in Utah and Arizona at elevations of {{convert|1800-3400|m|ft|sp=us|-2}}. They may flower starting in May or as late as September.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020f}} The variety is reported to intergrade with ''[[Aquilegia chrysantha]]'' (golden columbine) north of the grand canyon with [[Philip A. Munz]] observing it is very difficult to distinguish between dried specimens in herbariums.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=75}} When the variety was evaluated by NatureServe in 2004 they rated it as a vulnerable variety (T3) over the whole of its range and crititcally imperiled in the state of Nevada.{{sfn|NatureServe 2025f}}

===Names===
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea - Kirkton 1909.png|thumb|Illustrated by O.V. Kirkton, 1909{{sfn|Ramaley|1909|p=22}}]]
The genus name, ''Aquilegia'', is frequently said to mean 'eagle-like'. The author [[Bill Casselman (writer)|Bill Casselman]] strongly asserts this to be incorrect and that the name derives from the Latin adjective {{lang|la|aquilegus}} with the meaning 'drawing water'.{{sfn|Casselman|1997|pp=147–148}} The species name, ''coerulea'', is [[Botanical Latin]] meaning 'sky-blue' or 'dark-blue'.{{sfn|Casselman|1997|p=148}} Its name was sometimes spelled ''caerulea'', but ''coerulea'' was the spelling in the original description of the species.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=76}}

Two of the most frequently used [[common name]]s in English are ''Colorado columbine'' and ''Colorado blue columbine''.{{sfn|Duft|Moseley|1989|p=68}}{{sfn|Dodson|Dunmire|2007|p=6}} However, the species is also called ''Rocky Mountain columbine'' for its wider natural range,{{sfn|Busco|Morin|2010|p=50}} however this name is also infrequently applied to ''[[Aquilegia saximontana]]''.{{sfn|Nelson|1953|p=75}} The common name ''columbine'' is suggested to be related to the Latin for 'dove' for a resemblance of five doves drinking at a fountain.{{sfn|Dodson|Dunmire|2007|p=6}} ''Aquilegia coerulea'' is also known as ''blue columbine'' or simply as ''columbine'',{{sfn|Martin|1986|p=214}}{{sfn|Jones|1994|p=14}} though ''[[Aquilegia brevistyla]]'' is occasionally known as blue columbine and many species are casually called columbines such as ''[[Aquilegia canadensis]]''.{{sfn|Bush|1990|p=56}}{{sfn|Martin|1986|p=212}}

In the [[Gosiute dialect]] of the [[Shoshoni language]] ''Aquilegia coerulea'' is called either {{lang|shh|pa'-wa-gúmp}} or {{lang|shh|pa'-o-gûm-pi}} as recorded by [[Ralph Vary Chamberlin]].{{sfn|Chamberlin|1911|p=51}}

==Range and habitat==
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea - José Garrido 01.jpg|thumb|Meadow of ''Aquilegia coerulea'' in the [[San Juan National Forest]] ]]
Colorado columbine is native to the Rocky Mountains from northern New Mexico to southern Montana. It grows in all of the mountainous western half of Colorado and Wyoming. Additionally it grows in every county of Utah, parts of Nevada, and northern Arizona. It is listed by the USDA as growing in Idaho and South Dakota, but without county level distributions.{{sfn|NRCS 2025a}} The species was reported to grow in the [[Caribou–Targhee National Forest]] in eastern Idaho in 1973.{{sfn|Henderson|1978|p=173}} Though the geographic center of the range is in eastern Utah, the greatest number of plants is found in the mountains of north central Colorado.{{sfn|Prior|Busch|2021|p=2297}}

Rocky Mountain columbine grows in mountainous areas, starting at lower elevations with the [[Rocky Mountain Douglas-fir]] (''Pseudotsuga menziesii'' var. ''glauca''). They become more common at higher elevations in [[montane ecosystems]] and [[subalpine]] habitats with some growing above timberline in the [[alpine tundra]].{{sfn|Dodson|Dunmire|2007|p=6}} The lowest elevations where they grow naturally is {{convert|1800|m|ft|sp=us}} for ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''pinetorum'' and the highest is {{convert|3600|m|ft|sp=us}} for ''Aquilegia coerulea'' var. ''coerulea'' in the southern Rocky Mountains.{{sfn|Whittemore 2020f}}{{sfn|Whittemore 2020d}} This species of columbine grows poorly in heavy soils without good drainage. However, it prefers moist areas and is very often found in [[quaking aspen]] groves.{{sfn|USUE n.d.}} It will also grow in the open coniferous forests, meadows, and in clearings.{{sfn|Heil et al. 2013|p=882}} When there is sufficient water they will thrive on rocky outcrops and slopes.{{sfn|Pesman|1988|p=55}}

==Ecology==
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea - Robert Webster 01.jpg|thumb|Bumblebee visiting flower, [[Sangre de Cristo Wilderness]]]]
Within its natural range ''Aquilegia coerulea'' is most often pollinated by hawkmoths ([[Sphingidae]]) and bumblebees (''[[Bombus]]''). Specific species of hawkmoth include ''[[Hyles lineata]]'' and ''[[Sphinx vashti]]''.{{sfn|Brunet|Sweet|2006|p=238}} The bumblebees include ''[[Bombus appositus]]'', but ''[[Bombus flavifrons]]'' visit the flowers much more frequently. Hawkmoths visit most often during the night while bumblebees only visit during daylight hours.{{sfn|Brunet|Holmquist|2009|p=3745}} The plants have the highest rate of [[cross-fertilization]] between plants in areas where the number of flowers is smaller and the population of hawkmoths is highest.{{sfn|Brunet|Sweet|2006|pp=241–243}} Normally, only hawkmoths with their very long tongues are able access the nectar at the bottom of the very long spurs with bumblebees visiting primarily or only for pollen.{{sfn|Brunet|2009|p=1570}} Pale to white flowers pollinated by moths tend to have longer nectar spurs making nectar less available to bees.{{sfn|Dodson|Dunmire|2007|p=7}}

The [[western bumblebee]] (''Bombus occidentalis'') has been observed [[nectar robbing]] from ''Aquilegia coerulea'' by opening or using holes cut in the spurs.{{sfn|Pyke|1982|p=559}} Though prior to its decline in numbers in the 21st century they were also a significant pollinator of the species in some areas.{{sfn|Brunet|2009|p=1576}} The [[broad-tailed hummingbird]] also visit the blooms, but less frequently than moths or bees.{{sfn|Miller|1978|pp=407,410}}

===Conservation===
The conservation organization [[NatureServe]] reviewed the species in 1991 and found it to be [[NatureServe conservation status|globally secure]] (G5) due to being a widespread species that is very common in some areas.They also rated it as apparently secure (S4) in both Colorado and Wyoming. However they also rated it vulnerable (S3) in Montana and Nevada and imperiled (S2) in Arizona. They did not evaluate the rest of its range.{{sfn|NatureServe 2025a}} The wildflower authors [[Frank and John Craighead]] attributed a relative decline in the population of the species to [[overgrazing]] by sheep and cattle in 1963.{{sfn|Craighead|Craighead|Davis|1963|p=56}} Previously great numbers of the flowers were common with Payson writing in 1918: {{blockquote|While it is found in most of the Rocky Mountain states, it was chosen as the State flower of Colorado, and in that State it reaches its finest development. There, in the Canadian zone, among the aspens, spruces, and firs, it is not uncommon to see a hillside meadow so completely covered with ''A.&nbsp;caerulea'' as to hide all other vegetation and to make it seem a fairyland of huge, dancing, blue and white stars; nor is it a view soon to be forgotten.{{sfn|Payson|1918|pp=151–152}}}}

==Uses==
===Culture===
[[File:Colorado Statehood 13c 1977 issue U.S. stamp.jpg|thumb|upright=0.5|alt=A postage stamp with a perforated edge with the text "COLORADO" at the top and two snow covered mountains. Below is a columbine flower with the text "13¢ USA" next to it and the motto "The Centennial State" across the bottom.|Colorado USPS stamp, 1977]]
Though it is not the standard form for a columbine, the long spurs and shape of the Colorado columbine's flower is the most recognized in the genus.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=29}} The white and lavender variety of the columbine was officially instituted as the [[List of Colorado state symbols|flower symbol of Colorado]] on 4 April 1899 by an act of the [[Colorado General Assembly]]. In 1925 the species was protected by law in Colorado, preventing needless destruction or waste of the flowers. It prohibits the digging or uprooting Colorado columbine plants on public lands and prohibits picking more than twenty-five flowers, buds, or stems in one day.{{sfn|Colorado State Archives Staff|2025}} In 1930 Albert B. Sanford, Assistant Curator of History at the State Historical Society, wrote that the flower was chosen as the state flower with the blue representing the blue skies and, "the white represent the snowy ranges of the mountains, and the yellow, the gold that first attracted people here in 1858."{{sfn|Shankle|1938|p=333}} Three airplanes used by [[Dwight D. Eisenhower]], one as a General and the ''[[Columbine II]]'' and ''[[Columbine III]]'' as President, were named for the state flower of Colorado.{{sfn|Special to The New York Times|1954|p=1}} The name was selected by Eisenhower's grandchildren because of the connections of his wife, [[Mamie Doud]], to the state.{{sfn|Special to The New York Times|1954|p=25}} When a commemorative stamp for Colorado's centennial was finally issued in 1977 it prominently featured a Colorado columbine.{{sfn|Tower|1977|p=D37}}

In the 1890s there was also a group called the Columbine Association that sought to establish it as a symbol of the United States.{{sfn|Sargent|1898}} This campaign for the native columbines, including Colorado columbine and [[American columbine]], to be the national flower continued through the end of the 1920s,{{sfn|Robbins|1929|p=SM5}} but ultimately the rose was selected as the [[national flower of the United States]] in 1986.{{sfn|Associated Press|1986|p=A1}} It is used in the logos of the [[American Society of Plant Taxonomists]] as well as the Colorado Native Plant Society.{{sfn|Holmgren et al. 2012|p=66}}

Following the 1999 [[Columbine High School massacre]] in [[Columbine, Colorado]], the columbine became a symbol of remembrance in Colorado.{{sfn|Larkin|2007|pp=115–116}} {{As of|2023}}, a [[Vehicle license plates of the United States#Specialty plates|specialty license plate]] depicting a columbine in honor of the victims and survivors of the massacre was the most popular specialty plate in Colorado.{{sfn|Ortega|2023}}

In the [[traditional medicine]] of the [[Goshute]] people the seeds of Colorado columbine would provide a heart medicine. The seeds were chewed as a general medicine and a tea made from the roots was used for belly pain and for when patients were "sick all over".{{sfn|Chamberlin|1911|p=51}}

===Cultivation===
[[File:Aquilegia coerulea 'Crimson Star' Spectral comparison Vis UV IR.jpg|thumb|'Crimson star' in visible light, UV (showing [[nectar guide]]s), and infrared]]
The Colorado columbine is regarded as a very attractive species. The botanist Edwin Blake Payson wrote 1918, "''Aquilegia caerulea'' is without a doubt the most showy and splendid American species of this genus."{{sfn|Payson|1918|p=151}} It is used as an [[ornamental plant]] in gardens for cut flowers, in border beds, [[informal garden]]s, with roses or other shrubs, and in [[wildlife garden]]s.{{sfn|RHS Staff|2025}} It is planted in American gardens for its attractiveness to hummingbirds.{{sfn|Busco|Morin|2010|p=51}} It is also recommended as an attractive plant for revegitation of mountain roadsides in its native range by the Office of Natural Environment in the [[Federal Highway Administration]].{{sfn|Harper-Lore|Wilson|2000|pp=76, 171, 309, 329, 355, 501, 559}} The species was adopted as a garden plant very soon after its scientific description, with its introduction to the United Kingdom coming in 1864.{{sfn|Dodson|Dunmire|2007|p=6}}

Colorado columbine is winter hardy in [[USDA zone]]s 3–8,{{sfn|Busco|Morin|2010|p=50}} [[Royal Horticultural Society|RHS]] zone H5.{{sfn|RHS Staff|2025}} Plants in cultivation grow most successfully in average to medium garden soil that is well drained and even moisture. They may be grown by gardeners in full sun or partial shade, but plants grow best in light to moderate shade.{{sfn|Missouri Botanical Garden Staff|2025}} Compared to other columbine species it is more tolerant of heat and drought,{{sfn|Leatherbarrow|Reynolds|1999|p=29}} but is not as long lived in gardens.{{sfn|Schmid|2005|p=39}} It is propagated both by seed and the [[Division (horticulture)|division]] of plants.{{sfn|Busco|Morin|2010|p=51}} However, though plants divided in spring survive, they are slow to recover.{{sfn|RHS Staff|2025}}

Colorado columbine is vulnerable to [[powdery mildew]]s, particularly when its leaves are regularly wetted by overhead irrigation.{{sfn|Busco|Morin|2010|p=51}} It is also susceptible to infestation by [[aphid]]s,{{sfn|RHS Staff|2025}} though not more than other species of columbine.{{sfn|Nold|2003|pp=20–21}} Plants can also be attacked by [[leaf-miner flies]] and [[sawflies]].{{sfn|RHS Staff|2025}} Compared to other columbines it is attacked more often by the [[columbine leafminer]] (''Phytomyza aquilegivora''). The damage is unsightly, but not does not threaten the health of a plant.{{sfn|Johnson|2025}} They are tolerant of damage by deer and rabbits.{{sfn|Missouri Botanical Garden Staff|2025}}

The treatment of seeds with [[gibberellic acid]] and planting at {{convert|21|C|F}} results in 93% germination after three weeks. The seeds also display resistance to sprouting without treatment with none sprouting when planted at 21&nbsp;°C and 40% germination after two weeks after first being cold stratified at {{convert|4.5|C|F|0}}.{{sfn|Love|Akins|2018|p=270}} When grown in gardens plants will last three or four years a most and often are treated by gardeners like a biennial.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=18}} When planting large areas it is seeded at a rate of 112&nbsp;ounces per acre.{{sfn|Martin|1986|p=214}}

Its natural variability and ease of hybridization with other columbines is exploited in the selection of numerous [[cultivar]]s in different shades. This is a drawback to gardeners seeking to have standard blue and white columbines, often requiring them to seek collected seeds rather than replanting. The cultivars that are probably entirely or largely derived from Rocky Mountain columbine include 'Alba', 'Albiflora', and 'Snow Queen'. Cultivars that are thought to be significantly of ''A.&nbsp;coerulea'' ancestry include 'crimson star' and 'rose queen'.{{sfn|Nold|2003|p=79}} The 'Origami' series of hybrids such as 'Origami Blue', 'Origami White', and 'Origami Yellow' are listed by authors such as [[P. Allen Smith]] and [[Allan Armitage]] as being largely derived from ''A.&nbsp;coerulea'',{{sfn|Smith|2006|p=98}}{{sfn|Armitage|2008|p=112}} but plant sellers will sometimes list them as ''[[Aquilegia vulgaris]]''.{{sfn|Mygardenlife Staff|2024}} The 'Origami' hybrids were originally developed by Goldsmith Seeds.{{sfn|Fell|2013|pp=7, 144–145}} Hybrids with large numbers of flowers are significantly shorter lived. The practice of [[Deadheading (flowers)|deadheading]], removing fading flowers to prevent the plant from growing seeds, is used by gardeners to extend a plant's life and partly compensates for this.{{sfn|Jelitto et al. 1990|p=53}}

The genome of the 'Goldsmith' cultivar was sequenced as part of a study of the transfer of genes between columbine species and was later used to study the evolution of basal [[eudicots]].{{sfn|Filiault et al. 2018}}{{sfn|Aköz|Nordborg|2019}}

